# Expo + React Native Tech Stack Standards

This project uses a modern Expo and React Native stack. **When uncertain about any API or pattern, search official documentation first** rather than guessing or mixing incompatible approaches.

## Core Technologies

- **Expo SDK 54** - Latest Expo managed workflow
- **React Native 0.81.5** with **React 19.1.0**
- **NativeWind v4** (latest) - Tailwind CSS for React Native
- **react-native-safe-area-context 5.6.0** - Safe area handling
- **react-native-reanimated 4.1.1** - Performant animations
- **Bun** - Package manager (see bun.mdc rule)

---

## NativeWind v4 Usage

### Setup Pattern

```typescript
// tailwind.config.js - MUST use nativewind/preset
module.exports = {
  content: ['./App.{js,ts,tsx}', './components/**/*.{js,ts,tsx}'],
  presets: [require('nativewind/preset')], // Required for v4
  theme: { extend: { /* ... */ } }
};
```

### Styling Components

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - className prop
import { View, Text, Pressable } from 'react-native';

<View className="flex-1 bg-white p-4">
  <Text className="text-2xl font-bold">Hello</Text>
  <Pressable className="rounded-lg bg-blue-500 p-3">
    <Text className="text-white">Press me</Text>
  </Pressable>
</View>

// ❌ WRONG - Don't mix style prop with NativeWind
<View style={{ flex: 1 }} className="bg-white" />

// ⚠️ RARE - Only use style prop when dynamic or non-Tailwind values needed
<View style={{ transform: [{ rotate: `${angle}deg` }] }} />
```

### Custom Theme Values

```tsx
// Define in tailwind.config.js theme.extend
colors: {
  'calculator-bg': '#000000',
  'calculator-operator': '#ff9500',
}

// Use with className
<View className="bg-calculator-bg" />
<Text className="text-calculator-operator" />
```

### Conditional Styles

```tsx
// ✅ Template literals for dynamic classes
<Pressable className={`rounded-full p-5 ${isActive ? 'bg-blue-500' : 'bg-gray-500'}`} />

// ✅ Array join for multiple conditions
<View className={[
  'base-classes',
  isActive && 'active-classes',
  isDisabled && 'disabled-classes'
].filter(Boolean).join(' ')} />
```

